1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,037
Welcome to Module Three.

2
00:00:02,037 --> 00:00:05,175
In the field, you never see a genotype directly.

3
00:00:05,175 --> 00:00:06,725
You see phenotypes.

4
00:00:06,725 --> 00:00:10,275
Size, colour, behaviour, who survives the winter.

5
00:00:10,275 --> 00:00:13,762
And you have learned not to take appearances at face value.

6
00:00:13,762 --> 00:00:17,537
When you want to know what is really underneath, you design an assay.

7
00:00:17,537 --> 00:00:18,875
A common garden.

8
00:00:18,875 --> 00:00:20,787
A reciprocal transplant.

9
00:00:20,787 --> 00:00:22,387
A controlled cross.

10
00:00:22,387 --> 00:00:27,787
Today, the organism is a language model, and the phenotype is its confidence.

11
00:00:27,787 --> 00:00:32,325
Ask a model a question and it may say, I am ninety percent sure.

12
00:00:32,325 --> 00:00:34,225
That sounds like information.

13
00:00:34,225 --> 00:00:35,112
But is it?

14
00:00:35,112 --> 00:00:36,675
Here is the test.

15
00:00:36,675 --> 00:00:40,325
Collect every answer the model gave at ninety percent.

16
00:00:40,325 --> 00:00:42,962
Are about nine in ten of them actually right?

17
00:00:42,962 --> 00:00:47,112
If they are, the model is calibrated, and you can act on that number.

18
00:00:47,112 --> 00:00:52,525
If only six in ten are right, the model is overconfident, and the number is decoration.

19
00:00:52,525 --> 00:00:55,962
Notice that this is a different question from accuracy.

20
00:00:55,962 --> 00:01:00,287
A model can be right most of the time and still be badly calibrated.

21
00:01:00,287 --> 00:01:05,075
And for automation, calibration is often what matters most.

22
00:01:05,075 --> 00:01:11,025
If you know when a model is unsure, you can send those cases to a person and let the confident ones through.

23
00:01:11,025 --> 00:01:15,362
One of today's sources is a small open-source decision model called Kev.

24
00:01:15,362 --> 00:01:20,500
Its builders fit a single calibration setting on data the model never trained on.

25
00:01:20,500 --> 00:01:23,025
It does not change which answer wins.

26
00:01:23,025 --> 00:01:25,687
It only changes how sure the model sounds.

27
00:01:25,687 --> 00:01:32,562
On new data, that one adjustment cut its confident mistakes from about eight in a hundred to between two and three.

28
00:01:32,562 --> 00:01:38,662
They also test the model on questions that cannot be answered at all, to see whether it still claims to be sure.

29
00:01:38,662 --> 00:01:40,587
That is a trick worth stealing.

30
00:01:40,587 --> 00:01:46,950
The second idea today comes from Andrej Karpathy, and it is about reading outputs, not scoring them.

31
00:01:46,950 --> 00:01:54,125
Ask for explanations in a strict, simple controlled English, the kind written for aircraft maintenance manuals.

32
00:01:54,125 --> 00:01:56,012
Or ask for a diagram.

33
00:01:56,012 --> 00:01:57,837
Or a small web page.

34
00:01:57,837 --> 00:02:01,475
Short, literal statements are easier to check one at a time.

35
00:02:01,475 --> 00:02:04,350
And here is where the metaphor fails.

36
00:02:04,350 --> 00:02:10,475
A phenotype was shaped by selection, so on average it tells you something honest about the organism.

37
00:02:10,475 --> 00:02:16,650
A model's fluent, confident paragraph was shaped by training to be helpful and convincing.

38
00:02:16,650 --> 00:02:18,862
Fluency is not evidence.

39
00:02:18,862 --> 00:02:22,387
Confidence is not evidence either, until you have tested it.

40
00:02:22,387 --> 00:02:29,937
In the lab, you will have your coding agent write forty yes or no questions from your own field, plus five that cannot be answered.

41
00:02:29,937 --> 00:02:32,062
You will check the answer key yourself.

42
00:02:32,062 --> 00:02:38,937
Then a fresh model answers them with a confidence for each, and you get a reliability diagram as a little web page.

43
00:02:38,937 --> 00:02:43,437
Finally, you will gather this whole first unit into a notebook you can come back to.

44
00:02:43,437 --> 00:02:45,825
Open the lab, and start your assay.

